For years my computer has had problems I didn't understand. Games would stutter out of nowhere. Things would lag for no reason. I had no idea what was going on, so I did what everyone does: restart and hope. Sometimes it worked, as if by magic, and I never learned why.

Today I found one of those reasons. My Razer mouse software was quietly leaving dead programs behind, tens of thousands of them. Each one made Windows' graphics memory manager a little slower, until the whole machine stuttered, not just the game. A restart would have fixed it, and I would never have known.

## 2 fps, then stutter

Subnautica 2 had run smoothly the last time I played. Today it ran at 2 fps. My wife was streaming a movie from the media server on the same machine, so restarting was out. Instead I asked Claude Code, which runs in a terminal on my PC, to figure out what was wrong.

Getting from 2 fps back to a playable 30 was the easy part. The movie was being re-encoded on my GPU because of a subtitle track, and turning the subtitles off fixed that. But the constant stutter stayed. Then I noticed the terminal itself was hitching too, and that changed the question. This wasn't a game problem. Something underneath everything was stalling.

## The suspects

Every guess became a command that ran in seconds and either held up or didn't:

<SuspectList suspects={[
  { name: "A movie transcoding on the same GPU", evidence: "real: decode engine at 76%. Fixed it, 2 → 30 fps, still stuttering", guilty: false },
  { name: "Thermals", evidence: "CPU at 94 °C, but still boosting to 3.9 GHz. Not throttling", guilty: false },
  { name: "NVIDIA Overlay leaking 12.7 GB of memory", evidence: "real, and killed. Stutter unchanged", guilty: false },
  { name: "Frame pacing / G-Sync", evidence: "turned G-Sync on. Still stuttering, and the terminal was too", guilty: false },
  { name: "The Windows kernel itself", evidence: "System process burning 1.1–1.4 CPU cores nonstop, with no game-related reason", guilty: true },
]} />

## Tracing the kernel

Claude wrote a script that recorded 10 seconds of what the CPU was doing, including the kernel's call stacks, and I ran it as admin. Three quarters of the System process's time was going into a single function inside Windows' graphics memory manager:

```
dxgmms2.sys!VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf      75.28%
  ntkrnlmp.exe!NtUpdateWnfStateData          74.68%
    ntkrnlmp.exe!ExpWnfFindScopeInstance     74.30%   ← searching a list, one entry at a time
```

Why would a list search be slow? Because the list was enormous:

```
Live processes: 306   Process objects in kernel: 33,015   => zombies ~ 32,709
```

## 32,709 programs that no longer existed

When a program exits but something still holds a reference to it, Windows can't clean it up. It becomes a zombie. My PC had 306 programs running, and was still keeping track of 32,709 dead ones. Almost all of the references were held by Razer Game Manager, a background service that watches every program you launch so it can tell when you start a game. It apparently never lets go.

<ZombieGrid before={{ live: 306, zombies: 32709 }} after={{ live: 306, zombies: 260 }} />

Here is how that turns into a stuttering desktop:

<StallChain steps={[
  { plain: "A game (or anything on screen) needs graphics memory, and the GPU is getting full.", kernel: "dxgmms2.sys!VidMmWorkerThreadProc" },
  { plain: "Windows' graphics memory manager frees some up, and announces that it did.", kernel: "VIDMM_GLOBAL::HandleTrimWnf" },
  { plain: "To send the announcement, the kernel looks up an entry in a list, one item at a time.", kernel: "ExpWnfFindScopeInstance + memcmp", stat: "74% of System CPU" },
  { plain: "The list is bloated by every dead program Razer never let go of.", kernel: "33,015 process objects for 306 live processes", stat: "32,709 zombies" },
  { plain: "While that search runs, everything waiting on graphics memory waits too: the game, the desktop, my terminal.", kernel: "System process: ~1.25 cores busy" },
  { plain: "A restart clears the list. So restarting 'fixes' it, until it builds back up.", kernel: "481 hours of uptime ≈ 1,600 new zombies a day" },
]} />

Stopping the two Razer services cleared it within a minute, without a restart and without interrupting the movie. Zombies went from 32,709 to 260, and the System process from about 1.25 cores to 0.2. The stutter was gone.

(The chain is the measured stack plus my reading of it. I haven't seen Windows' source code, but the zombies and the CPU usage both dropped at the same moment, by the same factor.)

## Check yours

If your PC gets slower the longer it stays on, this takes one line in PowerShell:

```powershell
(Get-Counter '\Objects\Processes').CounterSamples[0].CookedValue - (Get-Process).Count
```

A few hundred is normal. Tens of thousands means something is leaking. `Get-Process | Sort HandleCount -Desc | Select -First 5` will usually tell you what. My gaming script now disables Razer's services and warns me if the count climbs again.

## Restart and pray

A few years ago I would have restarted and moved on, and this would have become one more piece of computer magic. It probably has been, many times, over the years: every stutter a reboot "fixed", and I never knew why.

I still couldn't have found this on my own. I didn't know xperf could print kernel stacks, that zombie processes were a thing I could count, or what `ExpWnfFindScopeInstance` is. What changed is that I don't need to. I bring what I know about my own machine: it was smooth before, the terminal stutters too, don't reboot. Claude turns that into commands, rules things out with data, and keeps going down a layer until the numbers explain it. It got four suspects wrong on the way. None of those mistakes cost more than a few seconds.
